  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1007 AM MDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nTODAY:\n\nThe low pressure system that has been situated over the region has \nsince moved out over the eastern plains. Because of this, generally \ndry and mild conditions are on tap for the better half of the day. \nAdditionally, today will be another day of below normal \ntemperatures, before warming up to more seasonable temperatures come \nthe weekend. Periodic gusty winds are possible in the westernmost \npart of our CWA today as the low exits the region. Afternoon gusts \nwill likely fall within the 30-35 mph range. Clearing skies tonight \nand this much colder airmass in place will promote effective \nradiational cooling and below freezing temperatures Thursday night \ninto Friday morning. Conditions will be most favorable across \nnorthwest Colorado and into some of the higher elevation valleys in \nthe San Juan and Central Colorado River Basins. Hence, the previous \nFreeze Watch has been upgraded to a Freeze Warning for those areas. \nWidespread temperatures of 25-30F are expected, with values dropping \nas low as 20F in favored spots. If you have sensitive vegetation or \noutdoor plumbing, now is a good time to take necessary precautions \nto protect them. \n\nFRIDAY + THE WEEKEND:\n\nBy Friday, high pressure will begin to nose in from the west, with \nan area of stationary low pressure situated over SoCal. This will \ncreate a Rex Block for the West, while deep troughing dominates the \neastern CONUS. This setup will keep conditions fairly stagnant for \nthe coming weekend, with generally light cloud cover and minimal \nprecipitation chances. Overnight lows Friday night into Saturday \nwill warm from the previous night, but will still be below freezing \nin some valley locations. Therefore, another Freeze Warning has been \nissued for the same locations as Thursday for the same time period. \nModels are hinting at some isolated showers over the San Juans/Four \nCorners area come Sunday afternoon as the low pressure begins to \nexit the Rex Block pattern. Although, there are still some \ndiscrepancies with timing and location so details are vague at \nthe moment. \n\nLastly, the next chance for active weather across eastern Utah and \nwestern Colorado is not expected until early to mid-next week. \nModels remain in poor agreement over most details regarding this \nnext system, so stay tuned.",
